.Sh CAVEATS
.Bl -bullet
.It
Units run concurrently on different threads, and a unit may run on any
worker.
State that two benchmarks share must be read-only while they run, or
thread-local; a read-only table that is filled lazily must be filled on the
calling thread before
.Fn xft_tailor_run .
.It
Concurrent units share the memory bus and the last-level cache, so
memory-bound benchmarks slow each other down, by an amount that depends on
which units run beside them.
Their absolute numbers vary from run to run; compare them through the ratios
of a group, and treat a small ratio as a tie, since load can move it too.
.It
The members of a group share one core and run one after the other, so each
sample starts with the caches as the previous member left them.
A member whose data does not fit in the caches together with the others'
loses some of it to them between its samples.
.It
The counters count user space only: the time a benchmark spends in the kernel,
in page faults or system calls, is in
.Fa ns
but not in
.Fa cycles ,
so the comparison by cycles leaves it out.
.It
.Fn xft_get_physical_cpu_count
takes half the logical CPUs, at least one.
Without simultaneous multithreading that is half the cores, and on a machine
that numbers the two threads of a core next to each other, 0 and 1, the
workers pinned to CPUs 0 to
.Va n-1
share cores.
.It
Every running unit holds a copy of each member's buffer set, so the peak
memory is about the worker count times the largest group times the largest
set queued.
.It
.Fa gb_per_sec
spreads the bytes reported over every sampled iteration, the dropped ones
included.
.It
.Fn xft_get_random_buffer
divides by the buffer count, which must not be 0.
.It
.Xr perf_event_open 2
fails when
.Pa /proc/sys/kernel/perf_event_paranoid
is above 2, and every job then fails.
.El
